1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:21,920
So last time we talked about what medicine does, and today I want to take a deep dive

2
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:29,520
into medical data, and I'm going to use as examples a lot of stuff from the MIMIC database,

3
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:34,280
which is one of the databases that we're going to be using in this class.

4
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:38,880
Some of you are probably familiar with it, and some of you are not.

5
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:43,640
And there are, I hope, some takeaway lessons from this discussion.

6
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:52,280
So for example, a few years ago when MIMIC 3 was about to be released, I was playing

7
00:00:52,280 --> 00:00:59,800
with the data, and I looked at the distribution of heart rates in the care view part of the

8
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:00,800
database.

9
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:08,240
So MIMIC, for those of you who don't know, has intensive care data from about 60-something

10
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:16,240
thousand admissions to intensive care units at the Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center

11
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:19,000
over a period of about 12 years.

12
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,960
And one of the technical difficulties that we encountered is that in the middle of that

13
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:29,960
time period, the hospital shifted from one information system that they used in their

14
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:32,360
intensive care unit to another.

15
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:36,800
Care view is the old one, medivision is the new one, and of course they're not exactly

16
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:40,480
compatible, so we'll see some examples of that.

17
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:44,360
So this is the old data, so this is from care view.

18
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:51,760
And you look at that and say, well, heart rates range from 40 to 200, roughly, which

19
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:53,480
is OK.

20
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:58,040
But then there's this funny thing, there are two peaks.

21
00:01:58,040 --> 00:02:08,240
So where, if ever, do you see two peaks in physiological data?

22
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:10,400
Not typical.

23
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:23,520
And so my initial reaction was, so then I looked a little closer and I said, hmm, what

24
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:27,560
do the heart rates look like from these two systems?

25
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:30,960
And if you look in care view, you see the picture that I just showed you, and if you

26
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:36,280
look in medivision, you see this other picture which looks more like what you would normally

27
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:38,640
expect.

28
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:43,920
And so I'm sitting there scratching my head, going, OK, there must be some difference between

29
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:44,920
these.

30
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:51,520
It's not that simultaneous with the switchover of the hospital from one information system

31
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:59,000
to another, physiology of people changed and all of a sudden some subset of people started

32
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,080
having faster heart rates.

33
00:03:03,080 --> 00:03:07,960
But if you think about that, what subset of people have faster heart rates?

34
00:03:07,960 --> 00:03:20,440
Yeah, kids.

35
00:03:20,440 --> 00:03:21,600
Right?

36
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:23,720
So I said, hmm, interesting.

37
00:03:23,720 --> 00:03:28,520
So anyway, if you look at the statistics, you see that the mean heart rate in care view

38
00:03:28,520 --> 00:03:35,280
is 108, and the mean heart rate in medivision is 87.

39
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:43,600
But of course, means are not that meaningful when you look at these bimodal distributions.

40
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:46,720
So then I said, well, what if we just look at adults?

41
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:55,480
So we look at people from age greater than one up to age 90, and I'll say a word about

42
00:03:55,480 --> 00:03:57,000
that in a minute.

43
00:03:57,120 --> 00:04:02,160
I look at those two distributions, they look pretty close, they look pretty similar.

44
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:09,600
So that means that the number of patients of different ages in the adult group is similar

45
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:12,440
in the two data sets.

46
00:04:12,440 --> 00:04:19,960
But if I don't exclude the very young or the very old, then I see this funny distribution

47
00:04:19,960 --> 00:04:24,880
where I have suppressed ages greater than 90, but not the young.

48
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:32,600
And what you see is that in care view, there's this giant spike at age zero, right?

49
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:39,520
So what happened at the hospital is that under the old system, it was also being used in

50
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:45,280
the NICU, the neonatal intensive care unit, and the new system was not being used in the

51
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:52,240
NICU, and therefore they didn't capture data about babies, right?

52
00:04:52,240 --> 00:05:01,160
And in fact, if you look at age versus heart rate of the entire population, you see two

53
00:05:01,160 --> 00:05:03,260
very peculiar things.

54
00:05:03,260 --> 00:05:08,920
So here are the adults that we've been talking about, and here are the babies, and sure enough

55
00:05:08,920 --> 00:05:11,240
they have higher heart rates.

56
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:17,600
And then here are these 300-year-old people who go, wow, I don't think I'm going to have

57
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:21,360
a heart rate when I'm 300 years old.

58
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:26,600
So who are those people?

59
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:29,080
Anybody have a clue?

60
00:05:29,080 --> 00:05:30,080
Yeah?

61
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:31,080
Entry errors?

62
00:05:31,080 --> 00:05:32,080
Sorry?

63
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:34,720
Entry errors?

64
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:36,080
There are too many of them.

65
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:41,600
Yeah, entry errors is always a possibility, but there's quite a few data points there.

66
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:42,600
Yeah?

67
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:45,400
Missing data, but it's 5,000, 300.

68
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:46,400
Close.

69
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:48,560
It's not quite missing data.

70
00:05:48,560 --> 00:05:55,520
So HIPAA, the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act, defines a set of criteria

71
00:05:55,520 --> 00:06:00,960
about protecting personal health information, and one of the things you're not allowed to

72
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:07,760
do is to specify the age of somebody who is 90 years old or older.

73
00:06:07,760 --> 00:06:14,200
And the reason is because the number of 97-year-olds is pretty small, and so if I tell you that

74
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:19,080
Willie is 97 years old, then you're going to be able to pick him out of a population

75
00:06:19,080 --> 00:06:24,800
relatively easily, and so it's prohibited to say that.

76
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:31,240
So as a result, everybody who's 90-year-older gets labeled as being 300 years old in the

77
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:32,240
database.

78
00:06:32,240 --> 00:06:35,040
It's an artifact.

79
00:06:35,040 --> 00:06:43,320
It's like back in my youth, I worked as a computer programmer at a health sciences computing

80
00:06:43,320 --> 00:06:53,760
facility at UCLA, and we used to have a convention that missing data was represented by 9999.

81
00:06:53,760 --> 00:07:00,120
And of course, if you average that into a real dataset, you get garbage, which people

82
00:07:00,120 --> 00:07:05,640
did regularly, so there are problems with this, and we're running into one of those.

83
00:07:05,640 --> 00:07:12,680
If you look at just the adults, the two systems actually look very similar.

84
00:07:12,680 --> 00:07:19,280
So the blue and red dots are the two systems, and I've drawn the trend lines between them,

85
00:07:19,280 --> 00:07:21,720
and you can see that they're very similar.

86
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:28,080
So it looks like as you get older, your heart rate declines very slightly, but it does so

87
00:07:28,080 --> 00:07:30,080
equally in the two datasets.

88
00:07:30,080 --> 00:07:31,080
Yeah.

89
00:07:31,080 --> 00:07:35,520
On the previous slide, beyond 300, it looks like there are people who are older than 300?

90
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:41,200
Well that's because the ages there are computed at the time that the heart rate is measured,

91
00:07:41,200 --> 00:07:47,440
and so if you are 300 years old when you admit it to the hospital, if you stay in the hospital

92
00:07:47,440 --> 00:07:53,460
for six months, then you're 300 and a half years old by the time of that measurement.

93
00:07:53,460 --> 00:08:01,320
So that's why there are data points to the right of 300, yeah, good catch.

94
00:08:01,320 --> 00:08:05,160
Okay, and then this is what the babies look like, and of course they do have higher heart

95
00:08:05,160 --> 00:08:07,000
rates.

96
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:08,680
And here are the oldsters.

97
00:08:09,320 --> 00:08:16,800
Actually there are people out to 310 years old because maybe they were discharged from

98
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:23,520
the hospital, and then at age 100 they came back, maybe they were 90 years old at the

99
00:08:23,520 --> 00:08:28,560
time they were initially admitted, 10 years later they came back, and we recorded more

100
00:08:28,560 --> 00:08:33,160
data about them, and so this is all relative to that 300.

101
00:08:33,160 --> 00:08:35,920
Okay, so that's just one example.

102
00:08:36,240 --> 00:08:42,200
The lesson there is be careful when you look at data because it can really easily fool

103
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:47,720
you, because there are all kinds of funny things about the way it's collected, about

104
00:08:47,720 --> 00:08:53,280
these artifactual things like 300-year-old patients, and so on.

105
00:08:53,280 --> 00:08:59,400
Okay, so here's a catalog of the types of data that are available to us.

106
00:08:59,400 --> 00:09:06,560
So we have typical kind of electronic health record data from hospitals, demographics,

107
00:09:06,560 --> 00:09:12,120
age sex, socioeconomic status, insurance type language, religion, living situation, family

108
00:09:12,120 --> 00:09:15,840
structure, location, work, etc.

109
00:09:15,840 --> 00:09:21,320
We have vital signs, your weight, your height, your pulse, respiration rate, body temperature,

110
00:09:21,320 --> 00:09:22,480
etc.

111
00:09:22,480 --> 00:09:27,760
So these are typically the things that if you ever go to a doctor's office or you go

112
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:34,240
into a hospital, a nurse will take you aside and weigh you and measure your height and

113
00:09:34,240 --> 00:09:39,080
check your blood pressure and take your temperature and stuff like that.

114
00:09:39,080 --> 00:09:45,200
These are standard vital signs, and so we have lots of those recorded.

115
00:09:45,200 --> 00:09:51,160
Medications, prescription medications, over-the-counter drugs, illegal drugs if you're willing not

116
00:09:51,160 --> 00:09:54,680
to lie to your health care provider.

117
00:09:54,720 --> 00:10:03,840
Again, one of my earliest days, I was hanging out with a cardiologist at Tufts Medical Center,

118
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:13,440
and we see this elderly lady who looks kind of terrible, and we're talking to her, well,

119
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:22,520
the doctor is talking to her, I'm trying to stay out of the way, and he says, so do

120
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:24,600
you drink alcohol?

121
00:10:24,600 --> 00:10:27,960
And she says, oh, no, never touch this stuff.

122
00:10:27,960 --> 00:10:35,680
And then we talk some more, and we go out of the patient's room, and the doctor turns

123
00:10:35,680 --> 00:10:42,120
to me out of earshot of the patient and says, oh, she's a chronic drunk, and said, well,

124
00:10:42,120 --> 00:10:43,120
how do you know?

125
00:10:43,120 --> 00:10:50,080
And he says, well, from lab tests, from the appearance of her skin, from her general demeanor,

126
00:10:50,720 --> 00:10:57,360
various ineffable factors, and so patients lie.

127
00:10:57,360 --> 00:11:01,960
They really do, because they don't want to tell you things.

128
00:11:01,960 --> 00:11:05,160
OK, medications, by the way, is a big deal.

129
00:11:05,160 --> 00:11:13,360
So there's this whole field called med rec, medication reconciliation, which is the hospital

130
00:11:13,360 --> 00:11:19,320
or the doctor's office's attempt to figure out what medications you're actually taking.

131
00:11:19,320 --> 00:11:25,240
So I'm a member of the MIT health plan, and if I sign into my health plan account, it

132
00:11:25,240 --> 00:11:34,040
tells me that I'm taking some pills that I got 12 years ago as part of a laboratory

133
00:11:34,040 --> 00:11:41,360
test, where I took two pills, which were supposed to have some physiological effect,

134
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:43,040
and then they measured that.

135
00:11:43,040 --> 00:11:48,800
And I've never gotten another pill and never taken one since then, nor would it be particularly

136
00:11:48,800 --> 00:11:52,360
good for me, but it's still on my record.

137
00:11:52,360 --> 00:11:56,840
And there's no notice of it ever having been discontinued.

138
00:11:56,840 --> 00:12:02,040
And that's a real problem, because if you're taking care of a patient, you'd like to understand

139
00:12:02,040 --> 00:12:06,720
what drugs they're actually taking, and it's hard to know.

140
00:12:06,720 --> 00:12:09,040
OK, then lab tests.

141
00:12:09,040 --> 00:12:16,360
So this is the things that you imagine that we do a lot of, and these are components of

142
00:12:16,360 --> 00:12:22,880
the blood and the urine, mainly, but also of the stool, saliva, spinal fluid, fluid

143
00:12:22,880 --> 00:12:30,000
taken off the belly, joint fluid, bone marrow, stuff coming out of your lungs.

144
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,120
It's anything, right?

145
00:12:32,120 --> 00:12:38,800
And any place where you can produce some specimen, they can send it to a lab and measure things

146
00:12:38,800 --> 00:12:44,760
in it, and they measure lots and lots of different kinds of things, and these are often useful.

147
00:12:44,760 --> 00:12:51,760
Pathology, qualitative and quantitative examination of anybody tissue, for example, biopsy samples

148
00:12:51,760 --> 00:12:56,040
or surgical scraps.

149
00:12:56,040 --> 00:13:01,440
If they do an operation, they cut something out of you, that typically winds up on a pathologist

150
00:13:01,440 --> 00:13:06,480
bench who then tries to figure out what its characteristics are, and that's, again, useful

151
00:13:06,480 --> 00:13:08,480
information.

152
00:13:08,960 --> 00:13:12,320
OK, microbiology.

153
00:13:12,320 --> 00:13:19,960
Ever since Pasteur, we know that organisms cause disease, and so we're quite interested

154
00:13:19,960 --> 00:13:28,720
in knowing what organisms are growing inside your body, and typically testing is not only

155
00:13:28,720 --> 00:13:34,800
to identify the organism, but also to figure out which antibiotics it's sensitive to and

156
00:13:34,800 --> 00:13:36,440
insensitive to.

157
00:13:36,560 --> 00:13:44,360
So you'll see things like reports of sensitivity testing at various dilutions.

158
00:13:44,360 --> 00:13:49,960
In other words, they try to give a strong dose of an antibiotic, a weaker dose, a weaker

159
00:13:49,960 --> 00:13:55,800
dose, a weaker dose, a weaker dose, to see which is the minimum level of dosing that's

160
00:13:55,800 --> 00:13:58,360
enough to kill the bacteria.

161
00:13:58,360 --> 00:14:05,800
There's a common missing there, but input-output of fluids is another important thing, because

162
00:14:05,800 --> 00:14:12,600
especially in the hospital, often get either dehydrated or overhydrated, and neither of

163
00:14:12,600 --> 00:14:17,240
those is good for you, and so trying to keep track of what's going into you and what's

164
00:14:17,240 --> 00:14:21,160
coming out of you is important.

165
00:14:21,160 --> 00:14:26,880
Then there are tons of notes, so an important one that we're going to look at in this class

166
00:14:26,880 --> 00:14:28,840
is discharge summaries.

167
00:14:28,840 --> 00:14:35,520
So these are the typically long notes that are written at the end of a hospitalization.

168
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:43,880
So this is a summary of why you came in, what they did to you, the main things they discovered

169
00:14:43,880 --> 00:14:50,440
about you, and then plans for what to do after your discharge, where are you going to go,

170
00:14:50,440 --> 00:14:54,600
what drugs are you going to be taking, when are you supposed to come back for follow-up,

171
00:14:54,600 --> 00:14:55,600
et cetera.

172
00:14:55,600 --> 00:15:01,760
I'll show you an excruciatingly long one of those later in the lecture today.

173
00:15:01,760 --> 00:15:09,120
But we also have notes from attendings under residents, nurses, various specialties, consultants.

174
00:15:09,120 --> 00:15:14,320
The referring physician, if somebody sends you to the hospital, that doctor will usually

175
00:15:14,320 --> 00:15:20,360
write a note saying, this is what I'm interested in, here's why I'm sending in the patient.

176
00:15:20,360 --> 00:15:25,720
There are letters back to the referring physician saying, okay, this is what we found out, here's

177
00:15:25,720 --> 00:15:28,960
the answer to the question you were asking.

178
00:15:28,960 --> 00:15:33,840
There are emergency department notes, so that's often the first contact between the

179
00:15:33,840 --> 00:15:36,440
patient and the healthcare system.

180
00:15:36,440 --> 00:15:43,440
So these are all important, and then there's tons and tons of billing data.

181
00:15:43,440 --> 00:15:52,560
So remember, the EHR systems were initially designed by accountants, and they were designed

182
00:15:52,560 --> 00:15:54,720
for the purpose of billing.

183
00:15:54,720 --> 00:16:01,600
And so we capture a lot of data about formalized ways of describing the condition of the patient

184
00:16:01,600 --> 00:16:07,920
and what was done to the patient in order to submit the right bills.

185
00:16:07,920 --> 00:16:14,440
You obviously want a bill for as much as possible, but you have to be able to justify the bills

186
00:16:14,440 --> 00:16:21,480
that you submit, because insurance companies and Medicare and Medicaid don't have a good

187
00:16:21,480 --> 00:16:22,880
sense of humor.

188
00:16:23,640 --> 00:16:29,200
If you submit bills for things that you can't justify, then you get penalized.

189
00:16:29,200 --> 00:16:31,840
Okay.

190
00:16:31,840 --> 00:16:36,240
And then there are administrative data, like which service are you on?

191
00:16:36,240 --> 00:16:40,480
So this is occasionally a confusing thing.

192
00:16:40,480 --> 00:16:46,420
You can go into the hospital and have heart problems, but it turns out that the heart-intensive

193
00:16:46,420 --> 00:16:51,600
care unit, the cardiac intensive care unit, is full up with patients.

194
00:16:51,600 --> 00:16:57,080
But there's an extra bed in the pulmonary intensive care unit, and so they stick you

195
00:16:57,080 --> 00:17:03,720
in that unit, but you're still on the cardiology service, and so there are these sort of mixture

196
00:17:03,720 --> 00:17:07,320
kinds of cases that you still have to take care of.

197
00:17:07,320 --> 00:17:13,520
Transfers are when you get transferred from one place to another in the hospital.

198
00:17:13,520 --> 00:17:14,640
Imaging data.

199
00:17:14,680 --> 00:17:22,120
So I'm not going to talk about that much today, but there are X-rays, ultrasound, CT, MRI,

200
00:17:22,120 --> 00:17:28,880
PET scans, retinal scans, endoscopy, photographs of like your skin and stuff like that, so

201
00:17:28,880 --> 00:17:31,480
this is all imaging data.

202
00:17:31,480 --> 00:17:36,520
And there's been a tremendous amount of progress recently in applying machine learning techniques

203
00:17:36,520 --> 00:17:42,680
to try to interpret the contents of these data, so these are also very important.

204
00:17:42,680 --> 00:17:45,920
And then there's the whole quantified self-movement.

205
00:17:45,920 --> 00:17:53,120
I mean, how many of you wear an activity tracker?

206
00:17:53,120 --> 00:17:54,440
Only about a third?

207
00:17:54,440 --> 00:17:59,080
I'm surprised at the place like MIT.

208
00:17:59,080 --> 00:18:06,640
So we measure steps and elevation change and workouts, and you can record vital signs and

209
00:18:07,480 --> 00:18:16,920
diet and your blood sugar, especially if you're diabetic, allergies, allergic incidents.

210
00:18:16,920 --> 00:18:23,240
There's all this mindfulness, mood, sleep, pain, sexual activity, and then people have

211
00:18:23,240 --> 00:18:29,720
developed this idea of N of 1 experiments where, for example, I had a student some years

212
00:18:29,720 --> 00:18:33,640
ago who was suffered from psoriasis.

213
00:18:33,640 --> 00:18:38,960
It's a proteic condition of the skin, and the problem is there are no good cures for

214
00:18:38,960 --> 00:18:44,200
it, and so people who suffer from psoriasis try all kinds of things.

215
00:18:44,200 --> 00:18:50,800
They stop eating nuts for a while, or they douse themselves with vinegar, or they do

216
00:18:50,800 --> 00:18:54,360
whatever crazy thing comes to mind.

217
00:18:54,360 --> 00:18:59,160
And we don't have a good theory for how to treat this disease, but on the other hand,

218
00:18:59,160 --> 00:19:04,280
some things work for some people, and so there's a whole methodology that has been developed

219
00:19:04,280 --> 00:19:09,000
that says when you try these things, act like a scientist.

220
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:18,000
Have hypotheses, take good notes, collect good data, be cognizant of things like onset periods

221
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:23,320
where you may have to drip vinegar on yourself for a week before you see any effect.

222
00:19:23,320 --> 00:19:27,160
So if it doesn't do anything after one day, don't stop.

223
00:19:27,160 --> 00:19:34,960
And furthermore, if you stop, then don't start something new immediately because you

224
00:19:34,960 --> 00:19:40,160
will then be confused about whether this is the effect of the thing you were on before

225
