00:38:30,360 --> 00:38:35,600
stuff as the flush solution, but this time not being used for flush.

464
00:38:35,600 --> 00:38:38,480
Metoprolol is a beta blocker.

465
00:38:38,480 --> 00:38:43,640
Here's another insulin, this time with an NDC code, et cetera.

466
00:38:43,640 --> 00:38:51,680
I love bag and vial, so these are not exactly medications.

467
00:38:51,680 --> 00:38:59,180
A bag is literally like a baggie that they put something into, and a vial is literally

468
00:38:59,180 --> 00:39:01,980
something that they put pills in.

469
00:39:01,980 --> 00:39:03,940
And why is that in the database?

470
00:39:03,940 --> 00:39:07,100
Because they get to charge for it.

471
00:39:07,100 --> 00:39:11,340
And I don't know what the charge is, but it wouldn't surprise me if you're paying five

472
00:39:11,340 --> 00:39:15,940
bucks for a plastic bag to put something in.

473
00:39:15,940 --> 00:39:23,900
Okay, so if we say, well, how many pharmacy orders are there per admission at this hospital?

474
00:39:23,900 --> 00:39:26,580
And the answer is a lot.

475
00:39:26,980 --> 00:39:34,260
If you look at, it's a very long tailed distribution, it goes out to about 2,500, but you see if

476
00:39:34,260 --> 00:39:41,900
I blow up just the numbers up to about 200, there's a very large number of people with

477
00:39:41,900 --> 00:39:52,780
like two prescriptions filled, and then a fairly declining number with more.

478
00:39:52,780 --> 00:39:54,300
And then it's a very long tail.

479
00:39:54,300 --> 00:40:01,900
So can you imagine 2,500 things prescribed for you during a hospital stay?

480
00:40:01,900 --> 00:40:05,980
Well, a little more about standards.

481
00:40:05,980 --> 00:40:13,380
So NDC is probably the best of the coding systems, and it's developed by the FDA.

482
00:40:13,380 --> 00:40:20,700
The picture up on the top right shows that the first four digits are the so-called labeler

483
00:40:20,780 --> 00:40:27,020
that's usually the person who produced the drugs or at least the person who distributes them.

484
00:40:27,020 --> 00:40:34,500
The second four digit number is the form of the drug, so whether it's capsules or tablets

485
00:40:34,500 --> 00:40:39,380
or liquid or whatever, and the dose.

486
00:40:39,380 --> 00:40:46,460
And then the last two digits are a package code which translates into the total number

487
00:40:46,460 --> 00:40:49,660
of doses that are in a package.

488
00:40:49,660 --> 00:40:56,500
So this is a godsend, and all of the robotic pharmacies and so on rely on using this kind

489
00:40:56,500 --> 00:41:00,620
of information nowadays.

490
00:41:00,620 --> 00:41:10,540
Unfortunately they ran out of four digit numbers, and so they added an extra digit, but they

491
00:41:10,540 --> 00:41:15,900
didn't do it systematically, and so sometimes they added an extra digit to the labeler and

492
00:41:15,900 --> 00:41:22,060
sometimes to the product code, and so there's a nightmare of translations between the old

493
00:41:22,060 --> 00:41:27,420
codes and the new codes, and you have to have a code dictionary in order to do it properly,

494
00:41:27,420 --> 00:41:28,420
and so on.

495
00:41:28,420 --> 00:41:35,460
Okay, well, if that weren't good enough, the International Council for the Harmonization

496
00:41:35,460 --> 00:41:41,700
of Technical Requirements for Pharmaceuticals for Human Use developed another coding system

497
00:41:41,700 --> 00:41:49,140
called MEDRA, which is also used in various places, and this is an international standard,

498
00:41:49,140 --> 00:41:54,460
which is of course incompatible with the NDC.

499
00:41:54,460 --> 00:42:03,180
There are also CPT is the Common Procedural Terminology, which we'll talk about in a little

500
00:42:03,180 --> 00:42:11,420
bit, and they have a sub-range of their codes which also correspond to medication administration,

501
00:42:11,420 --> 00:42:22,820
and so this is yet another way of coding giving medicines, and then the HCPCS is yet another

502
00:42:22,820 --> 00:42:32,220
set of codes for specifying what medicines you've given to somebody, and then I had mentioned

503
00:42:32,220 --> 00:42:37,060
this GSN number, which apparently the Beth Israel uses.

504
00:42:37,060 --> 00:42:42,780
This is a commercial coding system from a company called First Data Bank that is in

505
00:42:42,780 --> 00:42:48,900
the business of trying to produce standards, but in this case they're producing ones that

506
00:42:48,900 --> 00:42:55,300
are pretty redundant with other existing standards, but nevertheless for historical reasons or

507
00:42:55,300 --> 00:42:58,620
for whatever reasons people are using these.

508
00:42:58,620 --> 00:43:01,540
Okay, enough of drugs.

509
00:43:01,540 --> 00:43:04,940
So what procedures were done to a patient?

510
00:43:04,940 --> 00:43:08,180
If you look in MIMIC, there are three tables.

511
00:43:08,180 --> 00:43:16,780
There's procedures ICD, which has ICD-9 codes for about a quarter million procedures.

512
00:43:16,780 --> 00:43:25,100
There's CPT events, which has about half a million, 600,000 events that are coded in

513
00:43:25,100 --> 00:43:32,980
the CPT terminology, and then MEDA vision, the newer of the two systems, has about a

514
00:43:32,980 --> 00:43:37,740
quarter million procedure events that are coded in that system.

515
00:43:37,740 --> 00:43:44,420
So some examples, here's the most common ICD-9 procedure codes.

516
00:43:44,420 --> 00:43:55,220
So ICD-9 code 3893, of which there are 14,000 instances, is venous catheterization, not

517
00:43:55,220 --> 00:43:57,540
elsewhere classified.

518
00:43:57,540 --> 00:43:59,660
So what's venous catheterization?

519
00:44:00,420 --> 00:44:05,820
It's when somebody sticks an IV in your vein.

520
00:44:05,820 --> 00:44:06,820
Very common.

521
00:44:06,820 --> 00:44:14,020
You show up at a hospital before they ask you your name, they stick an IV in your arm.

522
00:44:14,020 --> 00:44:21,820
That's a billable event, too, an insertion of an endothracheal tube.

523
00:44:21,820 --> 00:44:27,260
If you're having any problems like that, they stick something down your throat, enteral

524
00:44:27,260 --> 00:44:30,820
infusion of concentrated nutritional substances.

525
00:44:30,820 --> 00:44:36,460
So if you're not able to eat, then they feed you through a stomach tube.

526
00:44:36,460 --> 00:44:40,300
So that's what that is.

527
00:44:40,300 --> 00:44:45,900
Continuous invasive mechanical ventilation for less than 96 consecutive hours.

528
00:44:45,900 --> 00:44:52,140
So this is being put on a ventilator that's breathing for you, et cetera.

529
00:44:52,140 --> 00:44:56,120
So you see that there's a very long tail of these.

530
00:44:56,120 --> 00:44:58,600
So those are the ICD-9 codes.

531
00:44:58,600 --> 00:45:05,980
Now CPT has its own procedure codes that go into tremendous amount of detail.

532
00:45:05,980 --> 00:45:12,600
So for example, this is the medicine subsection, and it shows you the kinds of drugs that you're

533
00:45:12,600 --> 00:45:23,040
being administered that are involved in, dialysis or psychiatry or vaccines or whatever.

534
00:45:23,040 --> 00:45:29,360
And then here are the surgical and the radiological codes, and there's tons and tons of detail

535
00:45:29,360 --> 00:45:30,360
on these.

536
00:45:30,360 --> 00:45:31,360
Yeah.

537
00:45:31,360 --> 00:45:34,840
So how can they put these codes as like 1,000 to 1,022?

538
00:45:34,840 --> 00:45:36,960
This seems really annoying for anyone.

539
00:45:36,960 --> 00:45:38,240
No, these are categories.

540
00:45:38,240 --> 00:45:49,640
So if you drill down, there's a fan out of that tree and you get down to individual codes.

541
00:45:49,640 --> 00:45:56,600
Just as a nasty surprise, CPT is owned by the American College of Physicians.

542
00:45:56,600 --> 00:46:07,080
And they could sue me if I showed you the actual codes because they're copyrighted.

543
00:46:07,080 --> 00:46:10,720
And you have to pay them if you use those codes.

544
00:46:10,720 --> 00:46:14,800
It's crazy.

545
00:46:14,800 --> 00:46:21,480
So if you look at the number of all of these codes per admission, you see a distribution

546
00:46:21,480 --> 00:46:28,560
like this, or if I separate them out, you see that there are more ICD-9 codes and fewer

547
00:46:28,560 --> 00:46:33,240
of the CPT and the codes that are in medivision.

548
00:46:33,240 --> 00:46:37,680
But they look somewhat similar in their distributions.

549
00:46:37,680 --> 00:46:40,040
Okay, lab measurements.

550
00:46:40,040 --> 00:46:46,600
So you send off a sputum sample, blood, urine, a piece of your brain, something.

551
00:46:46,600 --> 00:46:50,440
They stick it in some goo and measure something about it.

552
00:46:50,440 --> 00:46:52,240
So what is it that they're measuring?

553
00:46:52,240 --> 00:46:57,600
Well, it turns out that hematocrit is the most common measurement.

554
00:46:57,600 --> 00:47:05,440
So this is how much hemoglobin is in your blood or what fraction in your blood and is

555
00:47:05,440 --> 00:47:09,000
very important for sick people.

556
00:47:09,360 --> 00:47:16,160
The second most important is potassium and sodium creatine chloride, urea nitrogen, bicarbonate,

557
00:47:16,160 --> 00:47:17,160
et cetera.

558
00:47:17,160 --> 00:47:21,760
So this is a long, long list of different things that can be measured, and all this stuff

559
00:47:21,760 --> 00:47:24,040
is in the database.

560
00:47:24,040 --> 00:47:37,600
So for example, here's patient number two in the database, and on July 17th of 2138,

561
00:47:37,600 --> 00:47:42,960
this is part of the de-identification process to make it difficult to figure out who the

562
00:47:42,960 --> 00:47:45,280
patient actually is.

563
00:47:45,280 --> 00:47:57,800
This person got a test for their blood, and they reported atypical lymphocytes.

564
00:47:57,800 --> 00:48:02,120
So there are a couple of interesting things to note here.

565
00:48:02,120 --> 00:48:07,200
One is that some things have a value and others don't.

566
00:48:07,200 --> 00:48:12,080
So this is a qualitative measure, so there's no value associated with it.

567
00:48:12,080 --> 00:48:17,040
Just the fact of the label tells you what the result of the test was.

568
00:48:17,040 --> 00:48:21,600
The other thing that's interesting is this last column, which is LOINC, and I'll say

569
00:48:21,600 --> 00:48:28,200
a word about that in a minute, actually right now.

570
00:48:28,200 --> 00:48:33,840
So LOINC is the logical observation identifier's names and codes.

571
00:48:33,840 --> 00:48:41,320
It was developed by our colleagues at Reagan Street Clinic in Indiana about 15 years ago,

572
00:48:41,320 --> 00:48:44,440
maybe 20 years ago at this point.

573
00:48:44,440 --> 00:48:50,200
And the attempt was to say every different type of laboratory test ought to have a unique

574
00:48:50,200 --> 00:48:56,920
name, and they ought to be hierarchical so that if you have, for example, three different

575
00:48:56,920 --> 00:49:02,960
ways of measuring serum potassium, that they're related to each other, but that they're distinct

576
00:49:02,960 --> 00:49:08,680
from each other, because there may be circumstances under which the errors that you get from one

577
00:49:08,680 --> 00:49:13,360
measurement versus another are different.

578
00:49:13,360 --> 00:49:20,280
And so this is the standard way, if you send off your blood sample to a lab, they send

579
00:49:20,280 --> 00:49:27,960
back a string like this to the hospital or to your doctor's office that says it's coded

580
00:49:27,960 --> 00:49:38,240
in this OBX coding system, and here is the LOINC code, and this is the SNOMED interpretation,

581
00:49:38,240 --> 00:49:44,920
and so this string is the way that your hospital's EHR or your doctor's office system figures

582
00:49:44,920 --> 00:49:47,880
out what the result of the test was.

583
00:49:47,880 --> 00:49:54,640
HL7 is this 30-something-year-old organization that has been working on standardizing stuff

584
00:49:54,640 --> 00:50:00,360
like this, and LOINC is part of their standardization.

585
00:50:00,360 --> 00:50:05,800
So if you look at these, you say, well, again, how many tests per admission?

586
00:50:05,800 --> 00:50:14,160
Again a huge long tail, up to about 15,000 for a very small number of patients.

587
00:50:14,160 --> 00:50:22,400
If you look at lab tests per admission, you can do a log transform and get something that

588
00:50:22,400 --> 00:50:25,280
looks like a more reasonable distribution.

589
00:50:25,280 --> 00:50:30,720
By the way, that's a very generic lesson when we're going to do analyses of these data,

590
00:50:30,720 --> 00:50:37,240
is that often doing a transform of some sort, like in this case a log, takes some funny-looking

591
00:50:37,240 --> 00:50:43,000
distribution and turns it into something that looks plausibly normal, which is better for

592
00:50:43,000 --> 00:50:44,680
a lot of the techniques we use.

593
00:50:44,680 --> 00:50:45,680
Yeah.

594
00:50:45,680 --> 00:51:00,680
Yes, that's the whole idea of creating the standard.

595
00:51:00,680 --> 00:51:05,240
And that has been pretty successful, pretty successfully adopted.

596
00:51:05,240 --> 00:51:08,240
OK, chart events.

597
00:51:08,240 --> 00:51:13,320
So these are the things that nurses typically enter at the bedside.

598
00:51:13,320 --> 00:51:21,440
And so there are 5.1, 5.2 million heart rates measured in the MIMIC database.

599
00:51:21,440 --> 00:51:26,200
And Calprev-Flig is an artifact.

600
00:51:26,200 --> 00:51:32,080
It exists in every record, and it's some calibration, something or other that doesn't

601
00:51:32,080 --> 00:51:33,080
mean anything.

602
00:51:33,080 --> 00:51:36,200
I've never been able to figure out exactly what it is.

603
00:51:36,200 --> 00:51:41,200
SpO2 is the partial pressure of oxygen in your blood.

604
00:51:41,200 --> 00:51:45,440
If you use a pulse oximeter, that's what that's measuring.

605
00:51:45,440 --> 00:51:49,160
Respiratory rate, heart rhythm, ectopic type, dot, dot, dot.

606
00:51:49,160 --> 00:51:56,000
Now you might be troubled by the fact that here's heart rate again, right?

607
00:51:56,000 --> 00:52:03,520
But I've already shown you this, that heart rate in care view and heart rate in meta-vision

608
00:52:03,520 --> 00:52:11,040
were coded under different codes in the joint system that we created out of those two databases.

609
00:52:11,960 --> 00:52:20,200
And so you have to take care of figuring out what's what if you're trying to analyze this data.

610
00:52:20,200 --> 00:52:27,000
Not only do we have that problem of different age distributions across the two different data sets,

611
00:52:27,000 --> 00:52:32,720
but we also just have the mechanical problem that there will be things with the same label

612
00:52:32,720 --> 00:52:39,360
that may or may not represent the same measurement at different times in the system.

613
00:52:39,360 --> 00:52:48,000
OK, this is the number of chart entries per admission, again on a log scale.

614
00:52:48,000 --> 00:52:56,240
So you see that there are about 10 to the 3.5 chart entries per admission, so, you know,

615
00:52:56,240 --> 00:53:03,600
thousands of admissions, of chart events per admission.

616
00:53:03,600 --> 00:53:06,320
We also track outputs.

617
00:53:06,320 --> 00:53:14,960
So Foley-Catheter allows your bladder to drain without you having consciously to go to the bathroom,

618
00:53:14,960 --> 00:53:16,840
so they collect that information.

619
00:53:16,840 --> 00:53:25,080
There are 1.9 million recordings of how much fluid came out of your bladder.

620
00:53:25,080 --> 00:53:30,440
Chest tubes will drain stuff out of your chest if you have congestion.

621
00:53:30,480 --> 00:53:35,960
Medivision is if you pee regularly, stool out, et cetera.

622
00:53:35,960 --> 00:53:45,320
And again, I'm not sure I understand what the difference is between urine out Foley versus Foley.

623
00:53:45,320 --> 00:53:50,760
They may be the same thing, but one from Careview and one from Medivision.

624
00:53:50,760 --> 00:53:54,000
So again, typical kinds of problems.

625
00:53:54,000 --> 00:54:04,120
If you look at the number of output events per admission, you're seeing on the order of a hundred, roughly.

626
00:54:08,120 --> 00:54:13,680
Well, if you're tracking outputs, you should also track inputs, and so they do.

627
00:54:13,680 --> 00:54:22,080
And so D5W is this dextrose in water, 0.9% normal saline.

628
00:54:22,080 --> 00:54:33,160
People fall as an anesthetic, insulin, heparin, blood thinner, et cetera, fentanyl, I think an opioid, if I remember right.

629
00:54:33,160 --> 00:54:41,040
So these are various things that are given to people, and they affect the volume of the person.

630
00:54:41,040 --> 00:54:46,600
So this is an attempt to keep the person in balance and keep track of that.

631
00:54:46,600 --> 00:54:53,720
Medivision inputs are classified somewhat differently, but they have similar kinds of data.

632
00:54:53,720 --> 00:55:05,120
And if you combine them, you get, again, a distribution on a log scale that shows that there are on the order of 10 to the fifth input events.

633
00:55:05,120 --> 00:55:11,640
So quite a few input events because this is recorded periodically.

634
00:55:11,680 --> 00:55:14,200
Now, the paper that I had, yeah.

635
00:55:14,200 --> 00:55:16,040
Is that what's an input event again?

636
00:55:16,040 --> 00:55:18,520
Is that like when you come in the hospital and I was in it?

637
00:55:18,520 --> 00:55:21,440
No, no, no, it's an input into you.

638
00:55:21,440 --> 00:55:29,440
So it's like you drink a glass of water, the nurse is supposed to record it, although she doesn't always because she may not notice it.

639
00:55:29,440 --> 00:55:36,240
But if they hang an IV bag and pour a liter of liquid into you, they do record that.

640
00:55:36,240 --> 00:55:39,040
OK. All right.

641
00:55:39,040 --> 00:55:56,120
So I had you read this interesting paper and a discussion prior to that paper because one of the authors is a former student of mine and I know one of the other guys pretty well.

642
00:55:56,200 --> 00:56:20,120
And the former student, Zach Kohane, came back some years ago from a conference in California and was explaining to me that he ran into a venture capitalist who discovered that there's an interesting physiological variation in the abnormality of lab tests that are done at night.

643
00:56:20,120 --> 00:56:29,800
And he suspected that there's a diurnal variation that lab tests actually become more abnormal at night than they do during the day.

644
00:56:29,800 --> 00:56:39,480
And Zach, who is not only a computer science PhD, but also a practicing doctor, turns to him and says, you're an idiot, right?

645
00:56:39,480 --> 00:56:45,680
Who has their blood drawn at three o'clock in the morning?

646
00:56:45,720 --> 00:56:50,720
It's typically not healthy people, right?

647
00:56:50,720 --> 00:57:03,240
So this is another of these nice confounding stories where if you have a test done in the middle of the night, it probably indicates that you're sicker.

648
00:57:03,240 --> 00:57:14,880
So he and Griffin recruited their third author and went off and did a very large scale study of this question, which is what the paper that I asked you to read reports on.

649
00:57:14,880 --> 00:57:22,240
And so I said, well, I wonder if I could reproduce that study in the MIMIC database?

650
00:57:22,240 --> 00:57:31,320
And the answer, just in case you get your hopes up, was no, in large part because we just don't have the right kind of data.

651
00:57:31,320 --> 00:57:38,480
So there are not that many white blood counts that were measured in the MIMIC database, for example.

652
00:57:38,480 --> 00:57:49,520
But if you look at the, this is MIMIC data, and if you say, what's the fraction of abnormal white blood count values by hour?

653
00:57:49,520 --> 00:57:53,320
So this is midnight to midnight.

654
00:57:53,320 --> 00:57:59,400
And each hour, there's some fraction of these test results that are abnormal.

655
00:57:59,400 --> 00:58:20,600
And sure enough, what you see is that at 5 o'clock in the morning, a much higher fraction of them is abnormal than at 3 o'clock in the afternoon, which is consistent with Zach's peremptory comment about the guy being an idiot.

656
00:58:20,600 --> 00:58:29,520
So once again, I said, well, can we build a really simple model that predicts who's going to die in the hospital in this case?

657
00:58:29,520 --> 00:58:33,520
That's the easiest one to predict because I have that data.

658
00:58:33,520 --> 00:58:49,680
We could get three-year survival data, which is what they were looking at, but it's harder and it runs into censoring problems of what happens if the person was hospitalized less than three years before the end of our data collection period and so on.

659
00:58:49,680 --> 00:58:51,560
And so I avoided that.

660
00:58:51,560 --> 00:59:07,880
But what this is showing you is for each of the hours 0 to 24, what is the number of measurements?

661
00:59:07,880 --> 00:59:16,840
And for each of those hours, what is the fraction of those measurements that's abnormal?

662
00:59:16,840 --> 00:59:21,040
So I said, well, let's just throw it into a logistic regression model.

663
00:59:21,040 --> 00:59:30,040
And what comes out is something really weird, which is that a few particular hours are significant, but most of them are not.

664
00:59:30,040 --> 00:59:46,640
And that looks like noise to me, because you wouldn't expect that at 8 o'clock in the morning, the fact that you had something measured matters.

665
00:59:46,640 --> 00:59:52,520
But at 9 o'clock in the morning, that doesn't seem sensible.

666
00:59:52,520 --> 00:59:55,920
So I don't think there's enough signal here.

667
00:59:55,920 --> 01:00:13,800
And in fact, when I looked at the number of white blood count measurements at night and related to mortality, so false means people lived and true means they died, but you see that there's not a whole lot of difference between the distributions.

668
01:00:13,800 --> 01:00:24,280
But you also see that the number of white blood counts is relatively small in this database, and so I think we just don't have enough data to do it.

669
01:00:24,280 --> 01:00:38,080
On the other hand, if you look at a panel of different drugs, you look at mean values of blood urea nitrogen or calcium chloride, CO2, et cetera, you see that there is variation across time.

670
01:00:38,080 --> 01:00:55,240
So there is some sort of variance that's either caused by the diurnal physiology of the human body or by the routine practice of medicine about when people choose to take lab measurements.

671
01:00:55,240 --> 01:01:04,640
And in fact, if you look at the fraction of high and low lab values, they do vary by hour.

672
01:01:04,640 --> 01:01:23,680
And in particular, if you look at white blood counts, you see that the fraction of high values goes up at night and the fraction of low values goes down at night, which is consistent with what they saw as well.

673
01:01:23,680 --> 01:01:36,520
There's another way to measure it, which is instead of using normal ranges, the lab actually gives you a call that says, is this value normal, low, or high?

674
01:01:36,520 --> 01:01:38,680
And we can use that.

675
01:01:38,680 --> 01:01:47,160
That's a little bit more subtle because it depends on calibration of the equipment and is updated as the calibration changes.

676
01:01:47,160 --> 01:01:53,360
So that's probably a little bit more accurate, but you see essentially the same phenomenon here.

677
01:01:53,360 --> 01:02:12,600
But if you look at the distributions of when measurements are done that turn out to be normal versus when they turn out to be abnormal, there's a lot of similarity between the normal and the abnormal curves of when those measurements are taken.

678
01:02:12,600 --> 01:02:15,800
So we're not seeing that.

679
01:02:15,800 --> 01:02:19,000
OK, let me race through to the end.

680
01:02:19,000 --> 01:02:23,280
This is my heartbeat from my watch.

681
01:02:23,320 --> 01:02:30,480
You can actually download the stuff and put it in your favorite analysis engine and take a look.

682
01:02:30,480 --> 01:02:33,680
So here I was running across the Harvard Bridge.

683
01:02:33,680 --> 01:02:50,960
And if you look at my heart rate variability over the 30 seconds or so, you see that the interbeat interval ranges from about 550 to about 630 milliseconds, whatever, 20 milliseconds.

684
01:02:50,960 --> 01:02:58,640
And so you could calculate my heart rate variability, which is thought to be an indicator of heart health and so on.

685
01:02:58,640 --> 01:03:03,000
You can calculate that I was running at a pace of about 100.

686
01:03:03,000 --> 01:03:07,280
My heart was beating at a pace of about 100 beats per minute.

687
01:03:07,280 --> 01:03:11,600
So there's all sorts of information like that available.

688
01:03:11,600 --> 01:03:15,520
Now, as I said, I'm not going to get into this today.

689
01:03:15,520 --> 01:03:26,560
But this was a very successful recently published paper where they're able to take a look at images of the lung.

690
01:03:26,560 --> 01:03:30,960
So this is a transverse scan of the lung.

691
01:03:30,960 --> 01:03:37,840
And they have a deep learning machine that is able to identify these two yellow marked things,

692
01:03:37,920 --> 01:03:45,120
a pulmonary emboli, as opposed to these other things that are just random flecks in the tissue.

693
01:03:45,120 --> 01:03:47,840
And I can't do that by eyeball.

694
01:03:47,840 --> 01:03:51,280
Maybe a good radiologist might be able to.

695
01:03:51,280 --> 01:03:58,080
But this is claimed in the paper to outperform decent radiologists already.

696
01:03:58,080 --> 01:04:05,480
This was one of the articles that led Jeff Hinton to make this rather stupid pronouncement that said,

697
01:04:05,480 --> 01:04:13,520
tell your children not to become radiologists because the profession will be over by the time they get fully trained,

698
01:04:13,520 --> 01:04:20,720
which I don't believe they may do different things, but they won't go away.

699
01:04:20,720 --> 01:04:25,160
This was a slide from Ron Kikinis at the Brigham.

700
01:04:25,160 --> 01:04:34,040
And they're using automated techniques of analyzing white matter in order to identify lupus lesions.

701
01:04:34,120 --> 01:04:44,840
So lupus is a bad disease that shows up in these magnetic resonance images in certain ways.

702
01:04:44,840 --> 01:04:48,200
The last thing I want to talk about today is notes.

703
01:04:48,200 --> 01:05:00,400
So my students did a little exercise last semester where we tried to see how good is the average ape,

704
01:05:00,440 --> 01:05:07,280
namely, member of my research group at predicting mortality.

705
01:05:07,280 --> 01:05:11,160
And so we took a bunch of cases from the MIMIC data set,

706
01:05:11,160 --> 01:05:15,360
blinded to the question of whether the person lived or died.

707
01:05:15,360 --> 01:05:21,640
We gave the data to people in a kind of visualization tool, sort of like the one that I showed you earlier,

708
01:05:21,640 --> 01:05:27,520
that summarizes the case and then also gave people access to the notes,

709
01:05:27,520 --> 01:05:35,400
the de-identified notes about those cases to see whether people could predict better than a coin flip,

710
01:05:35,400 --> 01:05:39,080
whether somebody was going to live or die.

711
01:05:39,080 --> 01:05:42,840
And the answer is yes, slightly better, okay?

712
01:05:42,840 --> 01:05:46,120
Not immensely better, but slightly better.

713
01:05:46,120 --> 01:05:50,280
And furthermore, it looks like by giving them feedback.

714
01:05:50,280 --> 01:05:54,880
So as they're looking at these cases and trying to make the prediction,

715
01:05:54,920 --> 01:05:59,960
they make a prediction, you tell them if they were right or wrong, we learn.

716
01:05:59,960 --> 01:06:05,840
And so we get slightly better than slightly better than random, right?

717
01:06:05,840 --> 01:06:07,880
It's kind of interesting.

718
01:06:07,880 --> 01:06:16,000
Okay, so one of the things I discovered is that at least when I was playing the monkey in this exercise,

719
01:06:16,000 --> 01:06:24,680
I found the notes to be immensely useful, much more useful than the trend lines of laboratory data.

720
01:06:24,680 --> 01:06:27,360
Partly it's because I'm used to reading English.

721
01:06:27,360 --> 01:06:31,960
I'm not so used to reading graphs of laboratory data.

722
01:06:31,960 --> 01:06:38,640
But part of it is that there's a level of human understanding that is transmitted in the nursing notes

723
01:06:38,640 --> 01:06:44,280
and in the discharge summaries and so on that you don't get from just looking at raw data.

724
01:06:44,280 --> 01:06:49,760
And so there is very much the sense, which we're going to talk about in a couple of weeks,

725
01:06:49,800 --> 01:06:59,040
of how can we take advantage of that information, extract it, and use it in the kinds of modeling that we want to do.

726
01:06:59,040 --> 01:07:06,000
So in MIMIC, if you look, we have nursing notes and radiology reports and more nursing notes

727
01:07:06,000 --> 01:07:16,160
and electrocardiogram reports and doctor's notes and discharge summaries and echocardiograms, respiratory, etc.

728
01:07:16,160 --> 01:07:24,800
And if you look at the distribution of the length of these, these are unfortunately not on the same scale.

729
01:07:24,800 --> 01:07:29,880
But the discharge summary is the thing that's written at the time you leave the hospital.

730
01:07:29,880 --> 01:07:35,280
So this is sort of the summary of everything that happened to you during your hospitalization.

731
01:07:35,280 --> 01:07:48,240
And it's long, so it goes up to like 30,000 characters, it's a small, it's a short story, not so short, short story.

732
01:07:48,240 --> 01:07:53,480
Nursing notes tend to be shorter, they run up to about 3,000 characters.

733
01:07:53,480 --> 01:08:01,600
This other set of nursing notes, which I think comes from the other system, is a little bit longer, goes up to about 5,000.

734
01:08:01,600 --> 01:08:08,960
Doctor's notes are a little bit longer yet, they go up to about 10, 15,000 characters, typically.

735
01:08:08,960 --> 01:08:11,760
And there are various other kinds of notes.

736
01:08:11,760 --> 01:08:14,360
So I just wanted to show you a few of these.

737
01:08:14,360 --> 01:08:16,040
Here's a brief nursing note.

738
01:08:16,040 --> 01:08:20,960
So this is a patient who is hypotensive but not in shock.

739
01:08:20,960 --> 01:08:27,960
Patient remains on this drug drip at 0.75 micrograms per kilogram per minute.

740
01:08:27,960 --> 01:08:30,480
No titration needed at this time.

741
01:08:30,480 --> 01:08:33,080
Their blood pressure is stable at more than 100.

742
01:08:33,080 --> 01:08:38,160
The mean arterial pressure is 65, greater than 65.

743
01:08:38,160 --> 01:08:43,320
Wean them from this drug, presumably, if it's tolerated.

744
01:08:43,320 --> 01:08:48,960
Wound infection, so anterior groin area open and oozing.

745
01:08:48,960 --> 01:08:53,520
Moderate amounts of thin pink tinged serous fluid.

746
01:08:53,520 --> 01:09:00,400
Patient's stooling with small amounts of stool on something and dangerously close to the open wound.

747
01:09:01,040 --> 01:09:04,160
So this is sort of the nurse's snapshot.

748
01:09:04,160 --> 01:09:07,000
She just went in, saw the patient.

749
01:09:07,000 --> 01:09:15,520
By the way, I say she, but probably a vast majority of nurses in Boston area hospitals really are women.

750
01:09:15,520 --> 01:09:19,160
But there are some male nurses.

751
01:09:19,160 --> 01:09:24,040
And we'll record sort of a snapshot of what's going on with the patient.

752
01:09:24,040 --> 01:09:25,680
What are the concerns?

753
01:09:25,680 --> 01:09:31,400
In principle, this is going to be useful not only as a part of the medical record,

754
01:09:31,400 --> 01:09:36,480
but also when this nurse goes off shift and the next nurse comes on shift,

755
01:09:36,480 --> 01:09:43,160
then this is a recording of what the state of the patient was the last time they were seen by the nurse.

756
01:09:43,160 --> 01:09:50,720
In reality, the nurses tend to tell each other verbally rather than relying on the written version.

757
01:09:50,720 --> 01:09:56,560
I remember one time talking to a nurse in an intensive care unit in another part of the country,

758
01:09:56,560 --> 01:09:59,920
and I said, so whoever reads your notes.

759
01:09:59,920 --> 01:10:06,080
And she says quality assurance officers.

760
01:10:06,080 --> 01:10:15,840
So the hospital has people responsible for trying to assess the quality of care they're giving and lawyers when there's a lawsuit.

761
01:10:15,840 --> 01:10:26,480
And she was very happy because she had saved the hospital $10 million by having carefully recorded that some procedure had been done to a patient

762
01:10:26,480 --> 01:10:33,040
who then had a bad outcome and was suing the hospital for their neglect and not having done this.

763
01:10:33,040 --> 01:10:42,640
But because it was in the note that was proof that it actually had been done and therefore the hospital wasn't liable.

764
01:10:42,640 --> 01:10:46,080
So, but there's a lot of information in here.

765
01:10:46,080 --> 01:10:51,000
Now, I'm going to show you many pages of a typical discharge summary.

766
01:10:51,000 --> 01:11:07,600
So this is somebody on the surgery service who came in complaining of leg pain, redness and swelling secondary to infection of left femoral poplithal bypass.

767
01:11:07,600 --> 01:11:09,960
So she had surgery.

768
01:11:09,960 --> 01:11:11,800
I think she, yeah, female.

769
01:11:11,800 --> 01:11:15,320
She had surgery, which didn't heal well.

770
01:11:15,320 --> 01:11:37,440
So major surgical or invasive procedure, incision and drainage and pulse irrigation of the left groin and left above knee poplithal site incisions with exploration of bypass graft and excision of the entire left common femoral artery to above knee, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

771
01:11:37,440 --> 01:11:40,000
So this is what they did.

772
01:11:40,000 --> 01:11:41,920
History of the present illness.

773
01:11:41,920 --> 01:11:55,160
She's a 45 year old woman who underwent a left femoral AK doctor or something or other with PTFE, whatever that is, over a month ago on a certain date.

774
01:11:55,160 --> 01:12:04,320
By the way, these bracket asterisk things are where we've taken out identifying information from the record.

775
01:12:04,320 --> 01:12:09,680
She had been doing well postoperatively and was seen in the clinic six days prior to presentation.

776
01:12:09,680 --> 01:12:17,840
At this time, she acutely developed nausea, vomiting, fevers and progressive redness, swelling, pain of her left thigh, et cetera.

777
01:12:17,840 --> 01:12:21,200
So that's just page one of many pages.

778
01:12:21,200 --> 01:12:21,720
Yeah.

779
01:12:21,720 --> 01:12:22,240
Question.

780
01:12:22,240 --> 01:12:24,600
Is this completely these information fields?

781
01:12:24,600 --> 01:12:25,640
There is no help.

782
01:12:25,640 --> 01:12:29,360
There is no auto-complete if you type a patient's name or date.

783
01:12:29,360 --> 01:12:31,080
Not in this system.

784
01:12:31,080 --> 01:12:39,920
There are people, Henry Chouet at Mass General spent 10 years building a system that had auto-complete and so on.

785
01:12:39,920 --> 01:12:43,840
And some doctors liked it and some doctors hated it.

786
01:12:43,840 --> 01:12:50,320
And the MGH threw out all of their old systems in order to buy epic.

787
01:12:50,320 --> 01:12:51,440
And so it's gone.

788
01:12:51,440 --> 01:12:54,320
It was like 10 years of work down the drain.

789
01:12:54,320 --> 01:12:57,760
But it was not a spectacular success.

790
01:12:57,760 --> 01:13:03,720
Because whenever you have auto-complete, you have to anticipate every possible answer.

791
01:13:03,720 --> 01:13:05,240
And people are very creative.

792
01:13:05,240 --> 01:13:09,040
And they always want to type something that you can anticipate.

793
01:13:09,040 --> 01:13:11,680
So it's hard to support it.

794
01:13:11,680 --> 01:13:13,240
And what is Epic?

795
01:13:13,240 --> 01:13:21,640
Epic is a big company that has been winning all the recent contests for installing electronic medical record systems.

796
01:13:21,640 --> 01:13:26,800
Remember in my last lecture, I showed that we're reaching about 100% saturation.

797
01:13:26,840 --> 01:13:34,360
So they've been winning a lot of the installation deals.

798
01:13:34,360 --> 01:13:38,400
And they're getting a lot of the subsidy.

799
01:13:38,400 --> 01:13:45,400
The estimate I heard was that Partners Health Care, which is MGH of the Brigham and a couple of other hospitals,

800
01:13:45,400 --> 01:13:50,840
spent somewhere on the order of $2 billion installing the system.

801
01:13:50,880 --> 01:13:57,600
So that included all the customizations and all the training and all the administrative stuff that went with it.

802
01:13:57,600 --> 01:14:01,840
But that's a huge amount of money.

803
01:14:01,840 --> 01:14:03,120
OK.

804
01:14:03,120 --> 01:14:10,360
So we have past medical history, one pack a day smoker, abused cocaine.

805
01:14:10,360 --> 01:14:18,200
But she stopped six months ago, has asthma, type 2 diabetes, social history, family history.

806
01:14:21,840 --> 01:14:25,840
These are the physical exam results.

807
01:14:25,840 --> 01:14:31,840
So it's giving you a lot of information about the person.

808
01:14:31,840 --> 01:14:34,840
Description of the wound down at the bottom.

809
01:14:34,840 --> 01:14:36,840
Pertinent lab results.

810
01:14:36,840 --> 01:14:39,840
So these are copied out of the laboratory tables.

811
01:14:39,840 --> 01:14:41,840
Just double-tip with the drug results.

812
01:14:41,840 --> 01:14:42,840
Sorry?

813
01:14:42,840 --> 01:14:44,840
Just double-tip with the drug results, two slides back.

814
01:14:44,840 --> 01:14:45,840
Yep.

815
01:14:46,840 --> 01:14:52,840
So it has the fake dates of 2198 up there.

816
01:14:52,840 --> 01:14:53,840
Yeah.

817
01:14:53,840 --> 01:14:59,840
So the fact that there was a positive test in 2197 a year ago, so that's the medication.

818
01:14:59,840 --> 01:15:00,840
Yeah.

819
01:15:00,840 --> 01:15:07,840
The de-identification technology here maintains the relative dates, but not the absolute dates.

820
01:15:07,840 --> 01:15:21,840
So these are results, again, copied out of the laboratory database into the discharge summary,

821
01:15:21,840 --> 01:15:26,840
brief hospital course, and then a review of systems.

822
01:15:26,840 --> 01:15:27,840
So what's going on?

823
01:15:27,840 --> 01:15:32,840
Neurologically, cardiovascular, pulmonary, GI, GU, et cetera.

824
01:15:33,840 --> 01:15:38,840
Infectious disease, endocrine, hematology, prophylaxis.

825
01:15:38,840 --> 01:15:42,840
And at the time of discharge, the patient was doing well.

826
01:15:42,840 --> 01:15:51,840
No fever and stable vital signs, tolerating a regular diet, ambulating, voiding without assistance, and pain was well controlled.

827
01:15:51,840 --> 01:15:53,840
Medications on admission.

828
01:15:53,840 --> 01:15:56,840
So this was the medication reconciliation.

829
01:15:56,840 --> 01:15:58,840
Discharge medication.

830
01:15:58,840 --> 01:16:01,840
So this is what she's being sent home on.

831
01:16:01,840 --> 01:16:07,840
Discharge disposition is to the home with some follow-up service.

832
01:16:07,840 --> 01:16:11,840
And she's going home.

833
01:16:11,840 --> 01:16:19,840
And the discharge diagnosis is infected left femoral, popliteal, bypass graft, and the condition.

834
01:16:19,840 --> 01:16:25,840
And these are the instructions to the patient that say, you know, here's what you can do.

835
01:16:25,840 --> 01:16:30,840
Here's when you should come back and tell us if something is going wrong, et cetera.

836
01:16:31,840 --> 01:16:36,840
And here's what you should report if it happens.

837
01:16:36,840 --> 01:16:40,840
You know, if you have sudden severe bleeding or swelling, do this.

838
01:16:40,840 --> 01:16:43,840
Follow up with Dr. Somebody or other.

839
01:16:43,840 --> 01:16:47,840
Call his clinic at this number to schedule an appointment.

840
01:16:47,840 --> 01:16:53,840
And then follow up with Dr. Somebody else in two weeks.

841
01:16:54,840 --> 01:17:00,840
I think this is the same one.

842
01:17:00,840 --> 01:17:05,840
So just a couple of final words about standards.

843
01:17:05,840 --> 01:17:16,840
So you saw in David's introductory lecture a reference to Odyssey, which is a standard method of encoding the kind of data that we were talking about today.

844
01:17:16,840 --> 01:17:31,840
There's a likelihood that the next release of the MIMIC database will adopt the Odyssey formats rather than the, yeah, David's shaking his head, wondering why me too.

845
01:17:37,840 --> 01:17:45,840
Well, so, you know, what always happens is you say I'm going to adopt this standard asterisk with the following extensions.

846
01:17:45,840 --> 01:17:47,840
And that's probably what's going to happen.

847
01:17:47,840 --> 01:18:01,840
But it means that the central tables, you know, the ICD-9 code tables and the drug tables and things like that are likely to wind up adopting the formats of the Odyssey database.

848
01:18:01,840 --> 01:18:10,840
You should also know about this thing called FHIR, the Fast Health Interoperability Resources.

849
01:18:11,840 --> 01:18:28,840
So HL7 is the standards organization that had a tremendous success in the early 1990s in solving the problem of how to allow laboratories to report lab data back to the hospitals or the clinics that ordered the labs.

850
01:18:29,840 --> 01:18:40,840
And that character string with the up arrows and the vertical bars and so on that I showed you before that had link encoded in it is that standard.

851
01:18:40,840 --> 01:18:42,840
That's called HL7 version 2.

852
01:18:42,840 --> 01:18:45,840
It's still in use very widely.

853
01:18:45,840 --> 01:18:58,840
They then got ambitious and suffered second system syndrome, which is they decided to build HL7 version 3, which I used to teach in a class here 10 years ago.

854
01:18:58,840 --> 01:19:14,840
But one of my friends who works for a company that helps hospitals implement that sent me a like a 38 megabyte PDF file that describes what you need to know in order to implement that system.

855
01:19:14,840 --> 01:19:17,840
And as a result, nobody was doing it.

856
01:19:17,840 --> 01:19:33,840
So FHIR is a gross simplification of that that starts off and says, if a doctor refers a new patient to you, what is the minimum set of data that you need to know in order to take care of that person?

857
01:19:33,840 --> 01:19:38,840
And FHIR tries to provide just that subset of all the data.

858
01:19:38,840 --> 01:19:53,840
It has become a standard mainly because after Congress spent $42 billion or so bribing people into buying these information systems, they got mad that the information systems they bought couldn't talk to each other.

859
01:19:53,840 --> 01:20:05,840
And so they called in on the carpet the heads of these IT companies, health IT companies, and they yelled at them and they made them promise that there would be interoperability.

860
01:20:05,840 --> 01:20:08,840
They promised and out of that came FHIR.

861
01:20:08,840 --> 01:20:13,840
It was probably simultaneously developed, but they adopted it.

862
01:20:13,840 --> 01:20:25,840
And so now, in principle, it's possible to exchange data between different hospitals at least to the level of that degree of harmonization of the data.

863
01:20:25,840 --> 01:20:40,840
In reality, the companies don't want you to do that because they like there to be friction in not being able to take all your data to a different hospital because it is more likely to leave you at the one that you're at.

864
01:20:40,840 --> 01:20:53,840
So there's complicated socioeconomic kinds of issues in all this, but at least the standard exists and is becoming more and more widely deployed as long as Congress pays attention.

865
01:20:53,840 --> 01:20:57,840
It's ugly, so here's what a patient looks like, right?

866
01:20:57,840 --> 01:21:10,840
It's the usual unreadable XML garbage, but fortunately there are parsers that can turn it into JSON and simpler representations, and so that's pretty common.

867
01:21:10,840 --> 01:21:17,840
So the terminologies that exist are Loink and NDC, ICD-9 and 10.

868
01:21:17,840 --> 01:21:19,840
SNOMED I didn't talk about today.

869
01:21:19,840 --> 01:21:33,840
DSM-5 is the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual for Psychiatrists that's used as a common coding method for describing psychiatric disease, and there are many more of these.

870
01:21:33,840 --> 01:21:59,840
There's something called the Unified Medical Language Systems Metathosaurus from the National Library of Medicine that integrates about 180 of these different terminologies, and so there is a nice one-stop shop where you can get all these things from them.

871
01:21:59,840 --> 01:22:02,840
So take away lessons, know your data.

872
01:22:02,840 --> 01:22:18,840
Remember that first example of the heart rates that comes up over and over again, and doing machine learning and analysis on data that you don't understand is likely to lead you to false conclusions.

873
01:22:18,840 --> 01:22:27,840
Harmonization is difficult and time-consuming, and there are lots of things for which we just don't have standards, and so everybody develops their own representations.

874
01:22:27,840 --> 01:22:46,840
I had a PhD student about a decade ago who in his thesis wrote that he spent about half his time cleaning data, and I gave that thesis to another student who started a few years later who read it and he comes to me with just awestruck, and he says,

875
01:22:46,840 --> 01:22:50,840
what? He only spent half his time cleaning data.

876
01:22:50,840 --> 01:23:00,840
Unfortunately, that's roughly where we are in this field, so sorry to be a downer, but that's the current state of the art.

877
01:23:00,840 --> 01:23:10,840
And next time, David will start by looking at actually building some models with these kinds of data and showing you what we can accomplish.

